Roast.pet Weird Builder Projects =E2=80=94 4 useful projects

This run screened 25+ candidates across pet landmarks,
background/matting, sprite generation, browser video, captions, WebAR,
Etsy/POD tooling and mockup automation, while excluding projects
already surfaced in prior runs. Four new finds survived.

1) reearth/kamishibai =E2=80=94 REUSE NOW / BENCHMARK NOW

URL: https://github.com/reearth/kamishibai

Creator: Re:Earth / reearth

Recent activity: surfaced current within the last month

License: MIT

What it solves: deterministic web-page-to-video rendering without
forcing Roast.pet into a heavyweight video framework. The entire
contract is window.kamishibai =3D { meta, seek(ms) }. Any
React/DOM/canvas/WebGL page can therefore become the renderer. It
captures exact time-addressed frames through headless Chrome and
assembles them with ffmpeg.

Why this is unusually relevant: it supports incremental frame reuse by
hashing committed DOM state, parallel headless-Chrome capture,
narration/TTS prepasses, audio/video components, soft subtitle tracks,
burn-in subtitles, SRT output and an agent-facing kamishibai skill
authoring guide. It deliberately stays a mechanism rather than
becoming an editor/framework.

Install/demo route: npm i kamishibai react react-dom; expose
window.kamishibai; run kamishibai render .... Dependencies are Node,
Playwright/Chromium and ffmpeg. CPU only; parallel workers can
accelerate capture.

Evidence: runnable examples for React, video and narration; explicit
deterministic-render contract; frame caching; subtitle mux/burn-in;
MIT repository. Caveat: it does not promise cross-environment pixel
identity, so Chromium/fonts must be pinned.

Work replaced: potentially most of our custom render-loop, frame
capture, subtitle muxing, render caching and agent documentation.

Integration: LOW=E2=80=93MEDIUM. Maintenance risk: LOW=E2=80=93MEDIUM becau=
se it is
tiny (currently ~1 star), but the output boundary is ordinary
frames/video and therefore easy to replace.

Exact experiment: take the current canonical 15-second Roast.pet
HTML/canvas actor, implement only seek(ms), render at 30 fps, then
change one caption and rerender with incremental reuse. Compare total
LOC/render time against htmlrec/WebMotion/LeClap.

Verdict: REUSE NOW / BENCHMARK NOW.

2) tayles/ai-sprite-image-generator =E2=80=94 BENCHMARK NOW

URL: https://github.com/tayles/ai-sprite-image-generator

Creator: David Taylor / tayles

Recent activity: surfaced current within the last month

License: verify before embedding; surfaced metadata did not establish
license terms

What it solves: batching many named visual states into dense generated
sprite sheets and automatically splitting them back into individual
images. It uses Nano Banana Pro through kie.ai and explicitly supports
animals, avatars, game assets and product photos.

The economic trick is useful: instead of paying one image-generation
call per state, it asks for a 5x5 4K grid and splits the result into
25 cells. The author=E2=80=99s documented price comparison puts that at
roughly $0.0048/cell at the prices they observed. It also includes
parallel batches, API rate limiting, retries and named cell
definitions.

Install/API route: npm install ai-sprite-image-generator or bunx
ai-sprite-image-generator. Requires a KIE API key; no local GPU.

Evidence: package/CLI API, integration tests, examples for
cats/animals/product imagery, automatic image splitting, retries and
concurrency controls.

Work replaced: our first implementation of state batching, sheet
prompting, grid splitting, retries and concurrency.

Integration: LOW. Maintenance risk: MEDIUM because it depends on a
third-party inference wrapper/model naming.

Exact experiment: define cells neutral, blink, side-eye, stare,
recoil, mouth-A, mouth-E, mouth-O, mouth-M from one approved pet
reference. Compare cost, identity drift and usable-cell percentage
against generating those states individually. The key metric is cost
per accepted immutable state, not cost per raw image.

Verdict: BENCHMARK NOW; do not embed until license is verified.

3) CTDave001/automated_mockups =E2=80=94 REUSE NOW

URL: https://github.com/CTDave001/automated_mockups

Creator: CTDave001

Activity/maturity: 29 commits, ~69 stars, 18 forks

License: MIT

What it solves: deterministic product mockups without manually
entering placement coordinates for every scene. Templates contain a
known colored placeholder; the tool detects the placeholder=E2=80=99s
position, dimensions and rotation once, saves parameters to JSON, then
generates every design =C3=97 mockup combination in parallel.

Install/API route: pip install mockup-generator; run mockup-generator
calculate --hex-color ... once on template folders, then
mockup-generator generate .... Python 3.8+, NumPy, Pillow,
scikit-image, OpenCV; CPU only.

Evidence: CLI + Python API, tests, examples, rotation detection, nine
alignments, fit/fill/stretch modes, PNG/JPEG/WebP output and parallel
workers.

Work replaced: almost all fixed-scene mockup calibration and batch
composition for Roast.pet cards/T-shirts/posters. More importantly,
designers can author a mockup by painting a placeholder rather than
editing JSON coordinates.

Integration: LOW. Maintenance risk: LOW; the primitive is simple and
deterministic.

Exact experiment: make five Roast.pet canonical mockups (card on desk,
card in hand, T-shirt front, framed print, phone+card) with a #FF0000
placeholder. Run calculate once and then generate all five from 20
fake pet designs. Manually inspect perspective/rotation failures.

Verdict: REUSE NOW.

4) sov2000/etspi-cli =E2=80=94 WATCH / USE AS ESCAPE HATCH

URL: https://github.com/sov2000/etspi-cli

Creator: sov2000

Maturity: ~3 stars; surfaced active last month

License: GPL-3.0

What it solves: nearly the complete Etsy Open API v3 surface from a
shell: OAuth/token refresh, shop/listing reads, draft creation,
listing updates, inventory, images, videos, digital files and
properties. Crucially for Roast.pet, it can upload Etsy listing video
directly and transform listing JSON into draft/update-ready JSON.

Install route: pipx install etspi; Etsy API credentials required. It
persists/refreshes OAuth tokens and supports JMESPath shaping plus
JSON file I/O.

Evidence: explicit commands for image upload/ranking/alt text,
listing-video upload/update/delete, digital-file upload, inventory and
draft creation.

Why not adopt as core: GPL-3.0 and we already found more purpose-built
Etsy automation (etsykit). But this is an excellent operational/debug
CLI and a reference for API edge cases.

Work replaced: ad-hoc curl scripts and most manual Etsy API debugging.

Integration: LOW as a separate operator CLI. Maintenance risk: MEDIUM
because Etsy API behavior changes.

Exact experiment: against a disposable draft listing, use Etspi to
read listing JSON, upload one Roast.pet mockup, upload one 15-second
listing video, retrieve both, then delete/replace them. Keep it as the
manual escape hatch even if etsykit remains the production publisher.

Verdict: WATCH / USE AS ESCAPE HATCH, not embedded production code.

TOP 5 THINGS WE SHOULD NOT BUILD OURSELVES

- A bespoke deterministic browser capture loop =E2=80=94 benchmark Kamishib=
ai first.

- A frame cache/incremental rerender system =E2=80=94 Kamishibai already
fingerprints unchanged React DOM frames.

- Manual mockup placement coordinates for every product scene =E2=80=94 use
color-placeholder auto-calibration from automated_mockups.

- One expensive image-generation call per pet state =E2=80=94 benchmark den=
se
named sprite-sheet generation and split the cells.

- Etsy API debugging scripts =E2=80=94 Etspi already exposes almost the who=
le
API from the shell.

Most surprising hobby project

Kamishibai. One star, yet it has exactly the architectural property
Roast.pet needs: every frame is a pure function of time, with
incremental frame reuse, parallel capture, subtitles, narration
support and an agent-readable authoring skill. It is more interesting
to us than many 1,000-star generic video frameworks because it refuses
to become a framework.

Fastest integration win

automated_mockups. Paint a colored rectangle into five existing
product-scene images, run its calculator once, and Roast.pet
immediately gets deterministic batch listing images without
hand-calibrating coordinates.

Repo/author worth following

reearth/kamishibai. Its design philosophy=E2=80=94tiny deterministic contra=
ct,
ordinary web primitives, CI verification, agent-readable skill=E2=80=94is
unusually aligned with keeping Roast.pet=E2=80=99s custom kernel small.

Search coverage this run included GitHub projects around animal
landmarks/pose, sprite generation, browser matting/background removal,
WebAR/MindAR, WebCodecs/subtitles, Etsy API tooling, Printify/POD
workflows and product mockup generation. Previously surfaced projects
such as Alphaveil, BorderCut, PICORN, Light Converter, cat-filter,
dog_detection, ScratchLipSyncTool, MindAR and prior Etsy helpers were
intentionally not re-reported.
